/* data/detail-c/chem-11-01.js - GENERATED by tools/build.py from data/detail/chem-11-01.json (our own words). Do not edit. */
(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-11-01"] = {"chem-11-01-g01":{"idea":"A mole is a counting unit. Mass, number of particles and gas volume can all be converted to moles.","steps":["The mole relation is n = m / M. Here n is moles, m is the given mass and M is the molar mass.","m = 4.4 g and M = 44 g/mol.","n = 4.4 / 44 = 0.1 mol."],"others":"1.0 mol would need 44 g of CO₂. 10 mol would need 440 g. 0.01 mol would be only 0.44 g. All three come from slipping a decimal point.","trap":"Check the size: 4.4 g is one-tenth of 44 g, so the answer must be one-tenth of a mole.","answer":"0.1 mol"},"chem-11-01-g02":{"idea":"When water is added, the amount of solute stays the same. Only the volume changes, so the molarity falls.","steps":["Moles of solute stay constant, so M₁V₁ = M₂V₂.","M₁ = 0.5 M, V₁ = 20 mL, V₂ = 200 mL.","M₂ = (0.5 × 20) / 200 = 10 / 200 = 0.05 M.","Volume became 10 times bigger, so molarity became 10 times smaller."],"others":"0.1 M divides by 5 instead of 10. 0.5 M is the starting value, as if nothing was diluted. 5 M would mean the solution got stronger, which is impossible when water is added.","trap":"Dilution can only lower the molarity. Any answer larger than 0.5 M is wrong at once.","answer":"0.05 M"},"chem-11-01-g03":{"idea":"This is a limiting reagent question. The reagent that is used up first decides how much product forms.","steps":["Moles of AgNO₃ = 0.2 M × 0.050 L = 0.010 mol.","Moles of NaCl = 0.1 M × 0.050 L = 0.005 mol.","The reaction is 1 : 1, so NaCl runs out first. It is the limiting reagent.","Moles of AgCl = 0.005 mol.","Mass of AgCl = 0.005 × 143.5 = 0.7175 g, which is 0.72 g to 2 decimal places."],"trap":"Do not use the larger amount (AgNO₃). Always find the reagent that is finished first.","answer":"0.72 g"},"chem-11-01-b01":{"idea":"A mole is a counting unit. Mass, number of particles and gas volume can all be converted to moles.","steps":["n = m / M, with m = 8 g and M = 32 g/mol.","n = 8 / 32 = 0.25 mol."],"others":"0.5 mol would need 16 g of O₂. 2.5 mol and 4 mol are far too large, because they need 80 g and 128 g. Check that you divide the mass by 32, not multiply.","trap":"Use the molar mass of O₂ (32), not of one O atom (16).","answer":"0.25 mol"},"chem-11-01-b02":{"idea":"Mass and moles are linked through molar mass: m = n × M.","steps":["m = n × M, with n = 0.2 mol and M = 100 g/mol.","m = 0.2 × 100 = 20 g."],"others":"2 g and 0.2 g come from wrong decimal placing. 200 g is the mass of 2 mol, not 0.2 mol. A fifth of a mole must weigh a fifth of 100 g.","trap":"Moles to mass means multiply. Mass to moles means divide.","answer":"20 g"},"chem-11-01-b03":{"idea":"One mole holds 6.022 × 10²³ particles (the Avogadro number). Fewer moles means proportionally fewer particles.","steps":["Number of molecules = n × Nₐ.","n = 0.5 mol and Nₐ = 6.022 × 10²³ per mol.","Number = 0.5 × 6.022 × 10²³ = 3.011 × 10²³."],"others":"6.022 × 10²³ is the count for a full 1 mol. 1.204 × 10²³ is for 0.2 mol. 3.011 × 10²² is ten times too small, a power-of-ten slip.","trap":"Half a mole gives half of 6.022 × 10²³.","answer":"3.011 x 10^23"},"chem-11-01-b04":{"idea":"An ionic formula unit tells you how many ions each unit gives. NaCl gives one Na⁺ and one Cl⁻.","steps":["NaCl splits as NaCl → Na⁺ + Cl⁻.","So 1 mol of NaCl gives 1 mol of Na⁺.","0.1 mol of NaCl gives 0.1 mol of Na⁺."],"others":"1 mol would need 1 mol of NaCl. 0.05 mol wrongly halves the amount. 0.2 mol wrongly counts both Na⁺ and Cl⁻ together.","trap":"Count only the ion the question asks for.","answer":"0.1 mol"},"chem-11-01-b05":{"idea":"The Avogadro constant is the number of particles in one mole. It is a fixed number you must remember.","steps":["One mole of any substance contains 6.022 × 10²³ particles.","This number is called the Avogadro constant, Nₐ.","So Nₐ = 6.022 × 10²³ per mol."],"others":"6.022 × 10²⁴ and 6.022 × 10²² have the wrong power of ten. 3.011 × 10²³ is exactly half the real value, and is the count in half a mole.","trap":"Remember the power: 23, not 22 or 24.","answer":"6.022 x 10^23"},"chem-11-01-b06":{"idea":"At STP (standard temperature and pressure), one mole of any ideal gas takes 22.4 L.","steps":["n = V / 22.4, where V is the gas volume in litres.","V = 11.2 L.","n = 11.2 / 22.4 = 0.5 mol."],"others":"2 mol would fill 44.8 L. 0.25 mol would fill 5.6 L. 1 mol would fill the full 22.4 L. The given 11.2 L is exactly half of 22.4 L.","trap":"11.2 is half of 22.4, so the answer is half a mole.","answer":"0.5 mol"},"chem-11-01-b07":{"idea":"Mass percentage of an element = (mass of that element in one mole of compound / molar mass) × 100.","steps":["CH₄ has one C (12) and four H (4 × 1 = 4).","Molar mass = 12 + 4 = 16 g/mol.","% C = (12 / 16) × 100 = 75%."],"others":"25% is the share of hydrogen, not carbon. 12% uses the atomic mass 12 as if it were a percentage. 50% has no basis in the numbers.","trap":"The two percentages must add to 100: 75% C + 25% H.","answer":"75%"},"chem-11-01-b08":{"idea":"The law of conservation of mass says mass is neither created nor destroyed in a reaction. Mass of reactants equals mass of products.","steps":["Mass of solid + mass of gas = mass of product.","5.0 g + m = 8.0 g.","m = 8.0 − 5.0 = 3.0 g."],"others":"1.6 g comes from dividing instead of subtracting. 13.0 g adds the product to the solid, which is the wrong direction. 8.0 g is the whole product, not just the gas.","trap":"The gas adds the missing mass. Product minus solid gives it.","answer":"3.0 g"},"chem-11-01-b09":{"idea":"Significant figures are the digits that carry real measured information. Leading zeros do not count. Trailing zeros after a decimal point do.","steps":["The value is 0.00420.","The zeros before the 4 only show the decimal place, so they do not count.","The digits 4, 2 and the final 0 count. The final 0 is after the decimal point, so it shows precision.","Total = 3 significant figures."],"others":"5 counts the leading zeros wrongly. 2 drops the final zero, which does count here. 6 counts every digit including the zero before the decimal point.","trap":"Start counting from the first non-zero digit.","answer":"3"},"chem-11-01-b10":{"idea":"Volume units change by powers of ten. 1000 mL = 1 L.","steps":["Litres = millilitres / 1000.","250 mL / 1000 = 0.25 L."],"others":"25 L and 2.5 L multiply by the wrong power of ten. 0.025 L divides by 10000 instead of 1000. A bottle of 250 mL is a quarter of a litre.","trap":"Going from mL to L, the number gets smaller.","answer":"0.25 L"},"chem-11-01-b11":{"idea":"A N₂ molecule has two nitrogen atoms. So moles of atoms = moles of molecules × atoms per molecule.","steps":["Each N₂ molecule contains 2 N atoms.","Moles of N atoms = 0.3 × 2.","= 0.6 mol."],"others":"0.3 mol counts molecules and forgets that each has two atoms. 0.15 mol halves instead of doubling. 1.2 mol doubles twice.","trap":"Atoms and molecules are different things. Always multiply by atoms per molecule.","answer":"0.6 mol"},"chem-11-01-b12":{"idea":"Molar mass is the sum of the atomic masses of all atoms in the formula.","steps":["H₂SO₄ has 2 H, 1 S and 4 O.","Mass = 2 × 1 + 32 + 4 × 16.","= 2 + 32 + 64 = 98 g/mol."],"others":"82 g/mol uses only three oxygen atoms. 114 g/mol adds an extra 16, like five oxygen atoms. 49 g/mol is half of the right value, the equivalent mass.","trap":"Count the 4 in SO₄ as four oxygen atoms.","answer":"98 g/mol"},"chem-11-01-b13":{"idea":"Moles equal mass divided by molar mass. Use the molar mass of the whole molecule.","steps":["n = m / M, with m = 5.6 g and M = 28 g/mol.","n = 5.6 / 28.","n = 0.2 mol."],"trap":"N₂ has molar mass 28, not 14.","answer":"0.2 mol"},"chem-11-01-b14":{"idea":"Moles are the number of particles divided by the Avogadro number.","steps":["n = N / Nₐ, where N is the number of particles.","N = 1.505 × 10²³ and Nₐ = 6.022 × 10²³ per mol.","n = 1.505 / 6.022 = 0.25 mol."],"trap":"The powers of ten cancel, so only divide 1.505 by 6.022.","answer":"0.25 mol"},"chem-11-01-b15":{"idea":"The empirical formula is the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms. Convert percentages to moles, then divide by the smallest.","steps":["Take 100 g: C = 40.0 g, H = 6.7 g, O = 53.3 g.","Moles: C = 40.0/12 = 3.33, H = 6.7/1 = 6.7, O = 53.3/16 = 3.33.","Divide by 3.33: C : H : O = 1 : 2 : 1.","The empirical formula is CH₂O."],"others":"C₂H₄O₂ is a multiple of CH₂O, so it is a molecular formula, not the simplest ratio. CHO₂ and C₂H₆O do not match the 1 : 2 : 1 mole ratio found.","trap":"Empirical means smallest ratio. Always reduce it.","answer":"CH2O"},"chem-11-01-b16":{"idea":"The subscript in a formula gives atoms per unit. Fe₂O₃ has 2 iron atoms in every unit.","steps":["One mole of Fe₂O₃ contains 2 mol of Fe.","Moles of Fe = 0.25 × 2.","= 0.5 mol."],"others":"0.25 mol forgets the subscript 2. 1 mol and 0.75 mol multiply by 4 and 3, using the wrong number (the oxygen subscript is 3, but we want iron).","trap":"Use the subscript of the element asked, not of the other one.","answer":"0.5 mol"},"chem-11-01-b17":{"idea":"Moles of atoms of an element = moles of compound × subscript of that element.","steps":["C₆H₁₂O₆ has 6 O atoms per molecule.","Moles of O = 0.5 × 6.","= 3 mol."],"others":"6 mol is the value for 1 mol of glucose, not 0.5 mol. 1 mol and 0.5 mol ignore the subscript 6.","trap":"Scale the subscript by the moles you actually have.","answer":"3 mol"},"chem-11-01-b18":{"idea":"Mole fraction of a component is its moles divided by the total moles of all components.","steps":["Total moles = 2 + 3 = 5.","x_A = moles of A / total moles.","x_A = 2 / 5 = 0.4."],"others":"0.6 is the mole fraction of B, not A. 0.5 would mean equal moles of A and B. 0.25 has no link to the given numbers.","trap":"The mole fractions of A and B add up to 1: 0.4 + 0.6.","answer":"0.4"},"chem-11-01-b19":{"idea":"Mass percentage is the element's mass in one mole divided by the molar mass, times 100.","steps":["NH₃: N = 14, H = 3 × 1 = 3.","Molar mass = 14 + 3 = 17 g/mol.","% N = (14 / 17) × 100 = 82.4%."],"others":"17.6% is the share of hydrogen. 58.8% and 14.0% do not come from 14/17. Do not use 14 as a percentage.","trap":"Nitrogen is the heavy atom, so its share must be large.","answer":"82.4%"},"chem-11-01-b20":{"idea":"The law of definite proportions says a pure compound always has the same elements in the same mass ratio, whatever its source.","steps":["Both samples are pure water.","Both give the same H : O mass ratio of 1 : 8.","A fixed ratio in different samples of one compound is the law of definite proportions."],"others":"Conservation of mass talks about total mass before and after a reaction. Multiple proportions compares two different compounds of the same elements. Reciprocal proportions compares ratios across three substances.","trap":"Same compound, different source, same ratio means definite proportions.","answer":"Law of definite proportions"},"chem-11-01-b21":{"idea":"On dilution the moles of solute do not change, so M₁V₁ = M₂V₂.","steps":["M₁ = 2.0 M, V₁ = 100 mL, V₂ = 500 mL.","M₂ = (2.0 × 100) / 500.","M₂ = 200 / 500 = 0.4 M."],"others":"1.0 M would need the volume to double only. 0.2 M would need a volume of 1000 mL. 0.5 M has no basis in the numbers.","trap":"Volume grew 5 times, so molarity falls to one-fifth: 2.0 / 5 = 0.4.","answer":"0.4 M"},"chem-11-01-b22":{"idea":"In a balanced equation, the coefficients give the mole ratio. Convert grams to moles, use the ratio, then go back to grams.","steps":["Moles of H₂ = 4 g / 2 g/mol = 2 mol.","2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O: 2 mol H₂ gives 2 mol H₂O.","Mass of water = 2 × 18 = 36 g."],"others":"18 g is the mass of only 1 mol of water. 72 g uses 4 mol, which would need 8 g of H₂. 4 g is the mass of hydrogen itself, not of the water.","trap":"Hydrogen is 2 g/mol, not 1 g/mol, because it is H₂.","answer":"36 g"},"chem-11-01-b23":{"idea":"Molecular formula = (empirical formula) × n, where n = molar mass / empirical formula mass.","steps":["n = 180 / 30 = 6.","Multiply every subscript of CH₂O by 6.","Molecular formula = C₆H₁₂O₆."],"others":"C₂H₄O₂ is 2 × CH₂O, which has mass 60. C₃H₆O₃ has mass 90. C₉H₁₈O₉ has mass 270. Only C₆H₁₂O₆ has mass 180.","trap":"Check by adding up the mass of your final formula.","answer":"C6H12O6"},"chem-11-01-b24":{"idea":"Normality = molarity × n-factor. The n-factor of an acid is the number of H⁺ it gives per molecule.","steps":["N = M × n-factor.","M = 0.1 and n-factor = 2.","N = 0.1 × 2 = 0.2 N."],"others":"0.1 N ignores the n-factor. 0.05 N divides instead of multiplying. 0.4 N uses a factor of 4, which would be the number of oxygen atoms, not acidic H.","trap":"Normality is never less than molarity for acids and bases.","answer":"0.2 N"},"chem-11-01-b25":{"idea":"ppm means parts per million. It is mass of solute per million units of mass of solution.","steps":["1 kg = 1,000,000 mg.","ppm = (mass of solute / mass of solution) × 10⁶.","ppm = (5 mg / 1,000,000 mg) × 10⁶ = 5 ppm."],"others":"0.5, 50 and 500 ppm are all wrong by a power of ten. Since the units are mg per kg, the number stays the same.","trap":"mg per kg equals ppm directly.","answer":"5 ppm"},"chem-11-01-b26":{"idea":"Use the coefficients of the balanced equation as a mole ratio.","steps":["CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ has coefficients 1 : 1 : 1.","1 mol of CaCO₃ gives 1 mol of CO₂.","So 0.5 mol of CaCO₃ gives 0.5 mol of CO₂."],"others":"2 mol and 1 mol are bigger than the amount allowed by the 1 : 1 ratio. 0.25 mol halves the amount without reason.","trap":"When all coefficients are 1, moles stay equal.","answer":"0.5 mol"},"chem-11-01-b27":{"idea":"Gay-Lussac's law says gases at the same temperature and pressure react in simple whole-number volume ratios, the same as the coefficients.","steps":["N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃: volume ratio N₂ : NH₃ = 1 : 2.","V(NH₃) = 2 × V(N₂).","V(NH₃) = 2 × 10 = 20 L."],"others":"10 L would need a 1 : 1 ratio. 5 L divides instead of multiplying. 30 L is the volume of H₂ used, not NH₃ made.","trap":"Note that the volume of product is larger than N₂ here because the coefficient is 2.","answer":"20 L"},"chem-11-01-b28":{"idea":"Density is mass per unit volume, so mass = density × volume.","steps":["m = ρ × V.","ρ = 1.2 g/mL and V = 250 mL.","m = 1.2 × 250 = 300 g."],"others":"120 g does not use the volume correctly. 250 g ignores the density. 208.3 g comes from dividing 250 by 1.2, which is the wrong operation.","trap":"A density above 1 means the mass is larger than the volume number.","answer":"300 g"},"chem-11-01-b29":{"idea":"When adding or subtracting, the answer keeps as many decimal places as the number with the FEWEST decimal places.","steps":["12.11 has 2 decimals. 18.0 has 1. 1.012 has 3.","The fewest is 1, from 18.0.","Sum = 31.122, which is reported as 31.1 (1 decimal place)."],"others":"Using 2 decimal places because of 12.11 is wrong, because the rule uses the fewest, not the most. 3 follows the most precise number, which is also wrong. 0 wrongly treats the trailing zero in 18.0 as meaningless.","trap":"Addition looks at decimal places. Multiplication looks at significant figures.","answer":"1, set by 18.0 (fewest decimal places)"},"chem-11-01-b30":{"idea":"Molarity can be written with any matching units. 1 mol = 1000 mmol and 1 L = 1000 mL.","steps":["2 mol/L = 2 × 1000 mmol / 1000 mL.","The two factors of 1000 cancel.","So 2 mol/L = 2 mmol/mL."],"others":"0.002 divides by 1000 twice. 200 and 20 multiply by wrong powers of ten. Both conversion factors are 1000, so they cancel.","trap":"mol/L and mmol/mL are always numerically equal.","answer":"2 mmol/mL"},"chem-11-01-b31":{"idea":"The limiting reagent is the one with fewer moles when the reaction ratio is 1 : 1. Compare moles, not grams.","steps":["Moles of P = 10 / 40 = 0.25 mol.","Moles of Q = 10 / 20 = 0.5 mol.","The ratio is 1 : 1, so P (0.25 mol) is used up first.","P is the limiting reagent."],"others":"Q is in excess, because there are 0.5 mol of it and only 0.25 mol is needed. The reagents are not used up together because their moles differ. It can be determined from the given masses and molar masses.","trap":"Equal grams do not mean equal moles.","answer":"P"},"chem-11-01-b32":{"idea":"A homogeneous mixture looks uniform everywhere. A heterogeneous mixture shows separate parts.","steps":["Salt dissolved in water forms one uniform solution.","You cannot see separate salt and water parts.","So it is homogeneous."],"others":"Sugar and salt crystals, sand and iron filings, and oil on water all show visible separate parts or layers. They are heterogeneous mixtures.","trap":"Ask: can I see two parts? If yes, it is heterogeneous.","answer":"Salt dissolved in water"},"chem-11-01-b33":{"idea":"Empirical formula: change mass percentage to moles, then divide by the smallest number.","steps":["Take 100 g: C = 27.3 g, O = 72.7 g.","Moles C = 27.3/12 = 2.275. Moles O = 72.7/16 = 4.54.","Divide by 2.275: C : O = 1 : 2.","Empirical formula = CO₂."],"others":"CO would be a 1 : 1 ratio, which does not fit 1 : 2. C₂O₃ and C₃O₄ give ratios of 1 : 1.5 and 1 : 1.33, which do not match.","trap":"Divide by the smallest mole value, not the largest.","answer":"CO2"},"chem-11-01-b34":{"idea":"Molarity is moles of solute per litre of solution.","steps":["Convert volume: 200 mL = 0.2 L.","M = n / V = 0.4 mol / 0.2 L.","M = 2 M."],"others":"0.4 M ignores the volume. 0.08 M multiplies instead of dividing. 5 M divides the volume by the moles, which is upside down.","trap":"Always change mL to L before dividing.","answer":"2 M"},"chem-11-01-b35":{"idea":"Mass percentage = (mass of solute / mass of solution) × 100. Use the total mass of the solution.","steps":["Mass of solute = 4 g.","Mass of solution = 200 g (given).","% = (4 / 200) × 100 = 2%."],"others":"4% uses the solute mass as a percentage directly. 0.02% divides by 10000 by mistake. 20% shifts the decimal one place the wrong way.","trap":"Do not divide by the solvent mass (196 g).","answer":"2%"},"chem-11-01-b36":{"idea":"This is the law of multiple proportions. Take two elements that can join in different ways. Keep the amount of the first element the same. The amounts of the second element then match in a simple whole-number ratio.","steps":["CO and CO₂ are two compounds of C and O.","For fixed carbon, oxygen masses are in ratio 1 : 2.","A simple whole-number ratio like this is the law of multiple proportions."],"others":"Conservation of mass is about total mass in a reaction. Definite proportions deals with one compound only. Avogadro's law links gas volume and number of molecules, not mass ratios.","trap":"Two compounds of the same elements means multiple proportions.","answer":"Law of multiple proportions"},"chem-11-01-b37":{"idea":"Each formula unit gives a fixed number of ions. Multiply moles of salt by the number of that ion in the formula.","steps":["Al₂(SO₄)₃ contains 3 sulfate ions per unit.","Moles of SO₄²⁻ = 0.2 × 3.","= 0.6 mol."],"others":"0.2 mol ignores the 3 outside the bracket. 0.4 mol uses the aluminium subscript 2 instead. 0.8 mol multiplies by 4, counting oxygen atoms of one sulfate.","trap":"The number outside the bracket multiplies the whole ion.","answer":"0.6 mol"},"chem-11-01-b38":{"idea":"Ions of the same type add up. Total molarity of Cl⁻ = total moles of Cl⁻ / volume.","steps":["KCl gives 0.1 mol Cl⁻ and NaCl gives 0.1 mol Cl⁻.","Total Cl⁻ = 0.2 mol.","Volume = 500 mL = 0.5 L.","Molarity = 0.2 / 0.5 = 0.4 M."],"others":"0.1 M counts only one salt. 0.2 M forgets to convert 500 mL into 0.5 L. 0.5 M has no basis in the numbers.","trap":"Add the moles of both salts first, then divide.","answer":"0.4 M"},"chem-11-01-b39":{"idea":"First find moles from molarity and volume, then change moles into mass.","steps":["Moles = M × V = 0.2 mol/L × 0.5 L = 0.1 mol.","Mass = moles × molar mass.","Mass = 0.1 × 40 = 4.0 g."],"trap":"Use litres (0.5 L), not mL (500).","answer":"4.0 g"},"chem-11-01-b40":{"idea":"Diluting does not change moles of solute, so M₁V₁ = M₂V₂.","steps":["M₁ = 4.0 M, V₁ = 150 mL, V₂ = 600 mL.","M₂ = (4.0 × 150) / 600.","M₂ = 600 / 600 = 1.00 M."],"trap":"The volume became 4 times larger, so molarity becomes one-quarter.","answer":"1.00 M"},"chem-11-01-b41":{"idea":"For gases at the same temperature and pressure, the volume ratio equals the mole ratio from the balanced equation.","steps":["N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃: volume ratio H₂ : NH₃ = 3 : 2.","V(NH₃) = 15 × (2/3).","V(NH₃) = 10.0 L."],"trap":"H₂ is the reactant, so use 3 for H₂ and 2 for NH₃. The answer is smaller than 15 L.","answer":"10.0 L"},"chem-11-01-b42":{"idea":"ppm = mg of solute per kg of solution, since 1 kg = 10⁶ mg.","steps":["ppm = (8 mg / 10⁶ mg) × 10⁶.","The 10⁶ values cancel.","ppm = 8.0."],"trap":"mg per kg is the same number as ppm. No conversion is needed.","answer":"8.0 ppm"},"chem-11-01-b43":{"idea":"The limiting reagent is found by comparing moles with the ratio in the balanced equation, not by comparing the moles directly.","steps":["Moles AgNO₃ = 0.5 × 0.040 = 0.020 mol.","Moles BaCl₂ = 0.2 × 0.060 = 0.012 mol.","2AgNO₃ + BaCl₂: 0.012 mol BaCl₂ needs 0.024 mol AgNO₃.","Only 0.020 mol AgNO₃ is available, so it runs out first."],"others":"BaCl₂ is in excess: 0.012 mol is present but only 0.010 mol is needed. The reagents are not in exact proportion. The answer can be found, because the volumes and molarities are given.","trap":"Here BaCl₂ has fewer moles but is not limiting, because the ratio is 2 : 1.","answer":"AgNO3"},"chem-11-01-b46":{"idea":"Find the limiting reagent first. The excess reagent is what remains after the reaction.","steps":["Moles Al = 2.7 / 27 = 0.100 mol.","Moles CuSO₄ = 0.5 × 0.200 = 0.100 mol.","From 2Al : 3CuSO₄, 0.100 mol CuSO₄ reacts with 0.0667 mol Al.","Al left = 0.100 − 0.0667 = 0.0333 mol.","To 3 decimal places this is 0.033 mol."],"trap":"Both reagents have 0.100 mol, but the ratio is 2 : 3, so Al is in excess.","answer":"0.033 mol"},"chem-11-01-b44":{"idea":"Find the empirical formula from the percentages, then use the molar mass to scale it to the molecular formula.","steps":["Take 100 g: C = 85.7/12 = 7.14 mol, H = 14.3/1 = 14.3 mol.","Ratio C : H = 1 : 2, so the empirical formula is CH₂ (mass 14).","n = 56 / 14 = 4.","Molecular formula = C₄H₈."],"others":"C₄H₁₀ has the ratio 1 : 2.5, not 1 : 2. C₈H₁₆ has the right ratio but a mass of 112. C₂H₄ has the right ratio but a mass of only 28.","trap":"Always check the final mass against the given molar mass.","answer":"C4H8"},"chem-11-01-b45":{"idea":"To get molarity from a mass percentage, take 100 g of solution, find its volume from the density, then find the moles of solute.","steps":["In 100 g solution there is 20 g NaOH.","Moles of NaOH = 20 / 40 = 0.5 mol.","Volume = 100 g / 1.10 g/mL = 90.9 mL = 0.0909 L.","M = 0.5 / 0.0909 = 5.5 M."],"others":"11.0 M doubles the right value. 0.5 M is only the moles in 100 g, not moles per litre. 2.75 M halves the value, perhaps by using the wrong volume.","trap":"The density is above 1, so the molarity is more than the percentage-based guess.","answer":"5.5 M"},"chem-11-01-b47":{"idea":"Use the balanced equation as a mole ratio: 3 mol of Ca(OH)₂ react with 2 mol of H₃PO₄.","steps":["Ratio: Ca(OH)₂ : H₃PO₄ = 3 : 2.","Moles of H₃PO₄ = 0.3 × (2/3).","= 0.2 mol."],"others":"0.45 mol uses the inverted ratio 3/2. 0.3 mol assumes a 1 : 1 ratio. 0.6 mol doubles the amount, ignoring the 3.","trap":"The acid has the smaller coefficient, so it needs fewer moles.","answer":"0.2 mol"},"chem-11-01-b48":{"idea":"At STP, 22.4 L is one mole. So the molar mass of a gas follows from the mass of a known volume.","steps":["Moles = 2.24 / 22.4 = 0.1 mol.","Molar mass = 8.4 g / 0.1 mol = 84 g/mol.","n = 84 / 14 = 6.","Molecular formula = C₆H₁₂."],"others":"C₂H₄, C₃H₆ and C₄H₈ have molar masses 28, 42 and 56. None of these matches 84 g/mol.","trap":"The empirical formula alone does not give the real formula. You need the molar mass.","answer":"C6H12"},"chem-11-01-b51":{"idea":"In titration, compare the moles of each reagent using the coefficients of the balanced equation.","steps":["Moles Na₂CO₃ = 0.4 × 0.025 = 0.010 mol.","Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl: moles HCl = 2 × 0.010 = 0.020 mol.","M(HCl) = 0.020 mol / 0.050 L.","M(HCl) = 0.4 M."],"others":"0.8 M uses double the right moles and the wrong volume. 0.2 M ignores the 2 in front of HCl. 1.0 M has no basis in the numbers.","trap":"The 2 in the equation and the doubled volume cancel each other.","answer":"0.4 M"},"chem-11-01-b49":{"idea":"Find the limiting reagent, then use the equation ratio to find the product moles and mass.","steps":["Moles Pb(NO₃)₂ = 0.3 × 0.060 = 0.018 mol.","Moles KI = 0.5 × 0.040 = 0.020 mol.","Pb(NO₃)₂ needs 2 KI, so 0.018 mol needs 0.036 mol KI. KI is limiting.","Moles PbI₂ = 0.020 / 2 = 0.010 mol.","Mass = 0.010 × 461 = 4.61 g."],"trap":"The 1 : 2 ratio makes KI limiting even though it has more moles.","answer":"4.61 g"},"chem-11-01-b50":{"idea":"In neutralisation, use the equation's mole ratio between acid and base.","steps":["Moles NaOH = 0.25 × 0.0400 = 0.0100 mol.","H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH: moles H₂SO₄ = 0.0100 / 2 = 0.00500 mol.","M = 0.00500 mol / 0.0250 L.","M = 0.200 M."],"trap":"One H₂SO₄ needs two NaOH, so halve the moles of NaOH.","answer":"0.200 M"}};
